The patch for CVE-2019-9162 addresses an out-of-bounds memory access in the `fast_csum` function within `net/ipv4/netfilter/nf_nat_snmp_basic_main.c`, caused by insufficient ASN.1 length validation leading to array index errors.

The vulnerable code in `fast_csum` uses `memcpy` with `offset` to determine the size of the checksum buffer (`s`), but does not validate the `offset` value against the bounds of the input data, allowing potentially maliciously crafted packets to trigger out-of-bounds reads or writes.

### Patch Code:
```c
static void fast_csum(struct snmp_ctx *ctx, unsigned char offset)
{
    unsigned char s[12] = {0,};
    int size;

    if (offset & 1) {
        if (offset > 8) /* Validate offset to prevent buffer overflow */
            return;
        memcpy(&s[1], &ctx->from, 4);
        memcpy(&s[7], &ctx->to, 4);
        s[0] = ~0;
        s[1] = ~s[1];
        s[2] = ~s[2];
        s[3] = ~s[3];
        s[4] = ~s[4];
        s[5] = ~0;
        size = 12;
    } else {
        if (offset > 4) /* Validate offset to prevent buffer overflow */
            return;
        memcpy(&s[0], &ctx->from, 4);
        memcpy(&s[4], &ctx->to, 4);
        s[0] = ~s[0];
        s[1] = ~s[1];
        s[2] = ~s[2];
        s[3] = ~s[3];
        size = 8;
    }
    *ctx->check = csum_fold(csum_partial(s, size,
            ~csum_unfold(*ctx->check)));
}
```

### Explanation:
- The patch adds bounds checks on `offset` before using it to determine buffer size.
- If `offset > 8` in the odd case or `offset > 4` in the even case, the function returns early, preventing out-of-bounds memory access.
- This prevents exploitation via malformed SNMP NAT packets.

This fix was included in Linux kernel version 4.20.12 and later.

