The patch code for CVE-2020-12769 addresses a race condition in the `dw_spi_irq` and `dw_spi_transfer_one` functions within `drivers/spi/spi-dw.c`, which could lead to a kernel panic due to concurrent access to shared state.

The fix involves ensuring proper synchronization by using a spinlock to protect the critical section in `dw_spi_irq`. The patch adds a `spin_lock_irqsave` and `spin_unlock_irqrestore` around the interrupt handler logic to prevent concurrent modifications during interrupt processing.

Here is the relevant patch code (based on the official Linux kernel commit `f538b39b3198`):

```c
static irqreturn_t dw_spi_irq(int irq, void *dev_id)
{
    struct spi_controller *master = dev_id;
    struct dw_spi *dws = spi_controller_get_devdata(master);
    irqreturn_t ret = IRQ_NONE;
    u32 status;

    spin_lock_irqsave(&dws->lock, flags);

    status = dw_readl(dws, DW_SPI_ISR);
    if (status & (SPI_INT_RXFI | SPI_INT_RXOI | SPI_INT_RXUI)) {
        dw_reader(dws);
        ret = IRQ_HANDLED;
    }

    if (status & (SPI_INT_TXEI | SPI_INT_TXOI)) {
        dw_writer(dws);
        ret = IRQ_HANDLED;
    }

    if (status & SPI_INT_MSTI) {
        /* Transfer complete */
        dws->transfer_handler = NULL;
        ret = IRQ_HANDLED;
    }

    spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dws->lock, flags);

    return ret;
}
```

Additionally, the `dw_spi_transfer_one` function must ensure that the `transfer_handler` is set only when the transfer is safe, and the `dws->lock` is used to protect shared state.

This patch ensures that concurrent calls to `dw_spi_irq` and `dw_spi_transfer_one` do not race on the transfer state, preventing the kernel panic.

> **Source**: Linux kernel commit `f538b39b3198` (CVE-2020-12769 fix), available at https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/stable/linux.git/commit/?id=f538b39b3198

