The patch code for CVE-2020-12464 addresses the use-after-free vulnerability in the `usb_sg_cancel` function by ensuring proper reference counting before canceling USB transfers. The fix involves taking a reference to the URBs prior to initiating cancellation, preventing premature deallocation.

The patch (commit `056ad39ee925...`) modifies the `usb_sg_cancel` function to increment the reference count of the URBs before calling `usb_unlink_urb`, and ensures that the URBs are not freed while still in use.

**Patch code:**
```c
void usb_sg_cancel(struct usb_sg_request *io)
{
	unsigned long flags;
	int i, retval;

	spin_lock_irqsave(&io->lock, flags);
	if (io->status) {
		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&io->lock, flags);
		return;
	}
	/* shut everything down */
	io->status = -ECONNRESET;
	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&io->lock, flags);

	for (i = io->entries - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
		usb_get_urb(io->urbs[i]);  /* Take reference before unlink */
		usb_block_urb(io->urbs[i]);

		retval = usb_unlink_urb(io->urbs[i]);
		if (retval != -EINPROGRESS
		    && retval != -ENODEV
		    && retval != -EBUSY
		    && retval != -EIDRM)
			dev_warn(&io->dev->dev, "%s, unlink --> %d\n",
				 __func__, retval);
	}
}
```

This change ensures that the URBs remain valid during cancellation, mitigating the use-after-free condition. The fix was included in Linux kernel version 5.6.8.

