```c
static int __init fm10k_init_module(void)
{
    pr_info("%s - version %s\n", fm10k_driver_string, fm10k_driver_version);
    pr_info("%s\n", fm10k_copyright);

    /* create driver workqueue */
    fm10k_workqueue = alloc_workqueue("%s", WQ_MEM_RECLAIM, 0, fm10k_driver_name);
    if (!fm10k_workqueue) {
        pr_err("Failed to allocate fm10k workqueue\n");
        return -ENOMEM;
    }

    fm10k_dbg_init();

    return fm10k_register_pci_driver();
}
```

This patch ensures that if `alloc_workqueue` fails (returns `NULL`), the function returns `-ENOMEM` immediately without proceeding to register the PCI driver, thus preventing a NULL pointer dereference. The original code lacked this check, leading to a crash if memory allocation failed. The fix was included in Linux kernel version 5.0.11.

